{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "gcp-rag-app-alloydb-origin-v1",
  "title": "Document-grounded assistant with separate ingestion and evaluation paths",
  "subtitle": "A RAG application ingests files into a vector store and uses retrieved context for model responses, with a separate response-quality evaluation loop. Synthetic Azure reference; no deployment is implie",
  "description": "### Problem\nAn organization wants an assistant to answer questions using its own current documents and to measure whether generated responses are relevant and grounded.\n\n### Flow and Azure mapping\nUpload events trigger document processing. A pipeline extracts text, chunks content, creates embeddings, and indexes records in Azure AI Search. At request time, an application retrieves relevant chunks, packages context with the user question, calls a selected Azure model endpoint, applies response checks, and returns a response. An offline evaluation path stores prompts and response ratings for review and regression tests.\n\n### Trade-offs and assumptions\nThe Google source separates ingestion, serving, evaluation, and databases, and uses an AlloyDB pgvector store. Azure AI Search is a different retrieval service, not an equivalent database extension; index configuration, filtering, hybrid retrieval, embedding compatibility, and data lifecycle need separate choices. Evaluation does not guarantee factual answers, and document access controls must be enforced during retrieval.\n\nSource: [Google Cloud Architecture Center](https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/rag-capable-gen-ai-app-using-vertex-ai).\n\n## Source provenance\n\n- Origin provider: Google Cloud\n- Target: Azure conceptual adaptation\n- Primary source: [RAG infrastructure for generative AI using Agent Platform and AlloyDB for PostgreSQL](https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/rag-capable-gen-ai-app-using-vertex-ai)\n- Reviewed: 2026-11-06\n\n## Catalog status\n\nThis is an original, editable Azure conceptual adaptation. It is synthetic reference material, not a deployed system, certified vendor architecture, or automatic deployment plan.\n\n## Mapping caveats\n\n- Evaluation logs can contain sensitive prompts and responses; apply retention and access controls.",
